 Every event has an audience: either all members or only specific users. Direct message events go only to the two members of the conversation and never appear in the poll answer of a user outside the audience. A user with the invisible status appears offline to others. Two people with a block between them do not see each other's typing status.
 
-Automatic idle is per session and is kept only in the hub's memory, and there is no new API endpoint. If the client (`40-bosta.js`) sees no mouse, keyboard, touch, wheel or gamepad input on this device for the chosen time (10 minutes by default), it adds `idle=1` to the poll query, otherwise `idle=0`, and restarts the poll when the value changes. A device in a voice room is never counted as idle. In the desktop app the system idle time (`powerMonitor.getSystemIdleTime`) is counted too, and background windows decide by it alone. If all of a person's active sessions are idle and the status they chose is Online, the meta shows `status: 'idle'`, and the person sees "Idle (automatic)" on their own side. A chosen Idle, Do Not Disturb or Invisible status does not change, and the idle changes of an invisible person do not bump the meta version. When the shown status changes (poll, a new active session, a session that drops or closes), the meta version is bumped. Since every change triggers a meta broadcast to everyone, a session going idle is accepted at most once every 5 seconds (`idleGapMs`). An earlier change is ignored and the client reports it again on the next poll, and coming back from idle is applied right away. After a server restart the information comes back with the next poll. The decision is based on the difference of `Date.now()` values, not on the timer itself. Chrome can slow the timers of a background tab down to once a minute, so the decision may be up to about a minute late. The Idle Detection API is not used because it asks for permission and exists only in Chromium.
